WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 261: Henry David Thoreau: Walden Pond, Civil Disobedience, and the Simple Life

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:15.600
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series explores the quiet revolution of a solitary cabin in the Massachusetts Woods.

4
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:23.760
[Rebecca] The naturalist and rebel who taught the world that the only obligation one has the right to assume is to do at any time what one thinks right.

5
00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.600
[Rebecca] Here is George with the life of Henry David Thoreau.

6
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:45.520
[George] Born in July 1817 in the historic farming town of Concord, Massachusetts, David Henry Thoreau, who later transposed his names to Henry David, was born into a modest New England family that operated a small pencil manufacturing business.

7
00:00:45.520 --> 00:01:05.1000
[George] Surrounded by the meandering Concord and Sudbury Rivers, pine woodlots, and revolutionary battlefields, young Henry developed an almost mystical, intimate relationship with the natural world, spending hours skating on frozen ponds, fishing for pickerel, and observing the migratory arrivals of warblers and wildflowers.

8
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:21.920
[George] Entering Harvard College in 1833, Thoreau wore a green coat to chapel instead of the required black, immersing himself in classical Greek literature, German romanticism, and the ancient Hindu scriptures of the Bhagavad Gita and the Upanishads.

9
00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:31.840
[George] Graduating in 1837 during the financial panic that shuttered New England factories, Thoreau returned to Concord to work in his family's pencil factory.

10
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.440
[George] Applying his chemical knowledge of graphite and clay binding.

11
00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:50.160
[George] Thoreau engineered a revolutionary new pencil lead that produced no dust and did not smudge, making the Thoreau pencil the highest quality graphite instrument in the United States and winning prizes at the Massachusetts Charitable Mechanic Association.

12
00:01:50.160 --> 00:02:04.360
[George] Young Henry's deep intimacy with the woods, marshes, and waterways of Concord was nurtured by daily excursions through the New England countryside, observing the seasonal migrations of birds and the medicinal lore of Native American hunters.

13
00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:14.760
[George] His innovations in the family pencil factory revealed a sharp, practical mechanical intellect that complemented his classical Harvard education in Greek and Latin literature.

14
00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:26.360
[George] In the late 1830s, Thoreau formed a life-defining friendship with Ralph Waldo Emerson, who had recently settled in Concord and was igniting the American transcendentalist movement.

15
00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:40.920
[George] Emerson recognized in young Thoreau an authentic, uncorrupted American genius who lived the radical philosophical ideals that Emerson merely wrote about, inviting Thoreau to live in his household as a handyman, gardener and tutor.

16
00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:57.560
[George] Emerson gave Thoreau access to his vast library and encouraged him to keep a daily journal, a practice Thoreau maintained for 24 years, filling 39 dense manuscript volumes with over two million words of poetic natural observation and philosophical inquiry.

17
00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:11.880
[George] In 1838, Thoreau and his beloved older brother John founded the Concord Academy, an innovative school that abolished corporal punishment, incorporated daily nature walks, and taught conversational Greek and French.

18
00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:22.840
[George] The brother's deep bond was shattered in January 1842, when John accidentally cut himself while shaving and died in Henry's arms of a horrific lockjaw tetanus infection.

19
00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:32.600
[George] The grief-stricken Henry suffered sympathetic psychosomatic tetanus symptoms for weeks, retreating deeper into the solitary woods to find healing for his soul.

20
00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:46.640
[George] Emerson's generous mentorship provided Thoreau with a fertile intellectual environment, introducing him to Eastern Vedic philosophy and encouraging him to record his daily spiritual and natural observations in his vast personal journals.

21
00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:58.960
[George] The tragic death of his beloved brother John from Lockjaw was a devastating blow that deepened Henry's meditative solitude and intensified his quest to understand the underlying spiritual laws of nature.

22
00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:16.240
[George] On July 4, 1845, symbolically declaring his personal independence from social conformity, 27-year-old Henry David Thoreau walked into the pine woods bordering Walden Pond, a crystal clear, 61-acre glacial lake two miles south of Concord.

23
00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.880
[George] Situated on land owned by Emerson.

24
00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:39.040
[George] Borrowing an axe from Bronson Alcott to fell white pines, Thoreau built a simple ten by fifteen foot wooden cabin with his own hands, plastered with lime and sand, featuring a brick fireplace, two windows, a small desk, a bed, and three chairs, one for solitude, two for friendship, three for society.

25
00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:44.160
[George] The total construction cost was exactly $28 and twelve and a half cents.

26
00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:48.640
[George] Thoreau lived in the cabin for two years, two months and two days.

27
00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.600
[George] He famously explained his radical retreat.

28
00:04:51.600 --> 00:05:03.760
[George] I went to the woods because I wished to live deliberately, to front only the essential facts of life, and see if I could not learn what it had to teach, and not, when I came to die, discover that I had not lived.

29
00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:12.240
[George] I did not wish to live what was not life, living is so dear, nor did I wish to practice resignation, unless it was quite necessary.

30
00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:15.920
[George] I wanted to live deep and suck out all the marrow of life.

31
00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:42.840
[George] The construction of the simple cabin at Walden Pond was a deliberate declaration of philosophical independence, demonstrating that true wealth consists not in the abundance of possessions, but in the fewness of one's wants, in the quiet solitude of the conquered woods, Thoreau cultivated his garden of beans, bathed at sunrise in the crystal waters of the pond, and listened to the distant whistle of the locomotive, contemplating the cost of industrial progress.

32
00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:55.160
[George] In late July 1846, while walking from Walden Pond into Concord to pick up a mendied shoe from the cobbler, Thoreau was intercepted by local tax collector and constable Sam Staples.

33
00:05:55.160 --> 00:06:01.720
[George] Staples informed Thoreau that he was six years in arrears on his Massachusetts poll tax and must pay.

34
00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:17.240
[George] Thoreau firmly refused, he declared that paying the tax would constitute voluntary financial support for a federal government that was actively waging an aggressive, unconstitutional war of conquest against Mexico to expand the territory of human slavery.

35
00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:22.280
[George] Staples reluctantly arrested Thoreau and locked him in the Concord County jail.

36
00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:29.640
[George] That evening, an unidentified visitor, widely believed to be his Aunt Maria Thoreau, secretly paid the tax.

37
00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:37.640
[George] Staples released an exasperated Thoreau the following morning, and Henry immediately returned to the woods to lead a Huckleberry-picking party.

38
00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:43.400
[George] But the night in jail catalyzed one of the most influential political essays in world history.

39
00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:54.360
[George] In it, Thoreau articulated the immortal principle under a government which imprisons any unjustly.

40
00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:57.800
[George] The true place for a just man is also in prison.

41
00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:02.680
[George] He declared that the citizen must never surrender their moral conscience to the state.

42
00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:08.120
[George] The only obligation which I have a right to assume is to do at any time what I think right.

43
00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:24.000
[George] His refusal to pay the Massachusetts poll tax in 1846 was a principled act of moral protest against the expansion of slavery and the aggressive conquest of Mexico, proving that an individual's conscience is superior to unjust government mandates.

44
00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:34.800
[George] The essay Civil Disobedience became an immortal manifesto of nonviolent resistance, asserting that a just citizen must never surrender their moral agency to an oppressive majority.

45
00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:44.720
[George] In 1854, after seven painstaking revisions spanning nine years, Thoreau published his masterwork, Walden, or Life in the Woods.

46
00:07:44.720 --> 00:08:01.360
[George] Structured symmetrically around the cycle of the Four Seasons, Walden was simultaneously a practical manual of self-sufficient economics, a lyrical hymn to natural ecology, a searing social satire of commercial materialism, and a spiritual guide for the human soul.

47
00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:16.320
[George] Thoreau exposed the tragic absurdity of modern economic striving, observing that men spent their entire lives laboring under heavy mortgages, accumulating possessions that possess them, and trading their finite lifespans for frivolous luxuries.

48
00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:20.800
[George] Leading what he famously characterized as lives of quiet desperation.

49
00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:35.760
[George] Thoreau demonstrated that by simplifying one's physical wants to the bare essentials, a person could work only six weeks a year to meet their needs, freeing the remaining forty six weeks for reading, contemplation, friendship, and spiritual growth.

50
00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:49.440
[George] His philosophical motto rang across the centuries Simplify, simplify, instead of three meals a day, if it be necessary, eat but one, instead of a hundred dishes, five, and reduce other things in proportion.

51
00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:53.520
[George] Walden stands as an enduring literary and spiritual monument.

52
00:08:53.520 --> 00:09:03.080
[George] Challenging the commercial anxieties of modern life with its prophetic call to simplify, slow down, and discover the sacred miracle of the present moment.

53
00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:15.560
[George] Thoreau's acute ecological observations anticipated modern environmental science, documenting the interconnected balance of forest succession, wetland ecosystems, and wildlife habitats.

54
00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:31.480
[George] Beyond Walden Pond, Thoreau was a seasoned wilderness explorer, making extensive canoe journeys into the dense spruce wilderness of the main woods alongside Penobscot native guides like Joe Polis, and hiking the storm-swept coastal dunes of Cape Cod.

55
00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:46.440
[George] When abolitionist firebrand John Brown launched his armed raid on the Federal Arsenal at Harper's Ferry, Virginia, in October 1859, the entire nation, including Northern Republicans, condemned Brown as a dangerous lunatic.

56
00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:59.800
[George] Thoreau took the podium at Concord Town Hall and delivered his electrifying plea for Captain John Brown, becoming the first prominent intellectual in the United States to publicly defend Brown's character and moral courage.

57
00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:13.320
[George] Comparing Brown's impending execution to the crucifixion of Jesus Christ, Thoreau declared that Brown was a man of absolute moral conviction who was willing to sacrifice his life to liberate millions of enslaved human beings.

58
00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:21.080
[George] Thoreau's courage in standing alone before hostile crowds cemented his reputation as an uncompromising prophet of human freedom.

59
00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:33.720
[George] When John Brown was captured at Harper's Ferry, Thoreau's passionate, solitary defense of the abolitionist hero in Concord Town Hall galvanized northern intellectual support for the anti-slavery cause.

60
00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:45.680
[George] His extensive wilderness travels in the main woods, accompanied by Penobscot native guides, fostered in him a profound reverence for untouched primeval forests and indigenous ecological wisdom.

61
00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:58.800
[George] In December 1860, while counting tree rings on a freezing morning to study the succession of forest trees, Thoreau contracted a severe bronchial infection that reactivated his dormant tuberculosis.

62
00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:07.600
[George] Facing his impending death with sublime, serene tranquility, 44-year-old Thoreau spent his final months editing his manuscripts.

63
00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:15.600
[George] When an Orthodox aunt visited his bedside and asked if he had made his peace with God, Thoreau famously replied with peaceful wit.

64
00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:18.000
[George] I did not know we had ever quarreled.

65
00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:24.160
[George] When another friend asked if he could see anything of the next world, Thoreau whispered his last dying words.

66
00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:29.680
[George] Now comes good sailing, followed by the soft syllables, Moose, Indian.

67
00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:33.840
[George] He died peacefully in Concord on May 6, 1862.

68
00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:36.880
[George] At his funeral, Emerson eulogized him.

69
00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:42.000
[George] The country knows not yet, or in the least part, how great a son it has lost.

70
00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:51.760
[George] Thoreau's philosophy became the foundational inspiration for modern environmental conservation, wilderness preservation, and global nonviolent civil rights.

71
00:11:51.760 --> 00:12:00.160
[George] His doctrine of civil disobedience was adopted by Mahatma Gandhi to liberate India from the British Empire, and by Martin Luther King Jr.

72
00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:03.280
[George] to dismantle segregation in America.

73
00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:14.400
[George] Henry David Thoreau remains the ultimate compass of the human soul, reminding us that if a man does not keep pace with his companions, perhaps it is because he hears a different drummer.

74
00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:16.960
[George] Let him step to the music which he hears.

75
00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:19.360
[George] However measured or far away.

76
00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:33.080
[George] Facing his final illness with radiant serenity, Thoreau met death with the same quiet dignity and philosophical composure that defined his entire life, leaving behind a timeless legacy of moral courage and natural wonder.

77
00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:46.040
[George] His prophetic teachings inspired the global civil rights movements of Mahatma Gandhi and Martin Luther King J.R., proving that a solitary voice of truth can shake empires and awaken the conscience of the world.

78
00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:10.120
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

